      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2010 Elsevier Masson SAS. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "INTRODUCTION",
            "content": "Candida infection limited to the central nervous system is extremely rare, and may be confused with tuberculosis on the grounds of the clinical and cerebrospinal fluid findings."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "CASE REPORT",
            "content": "A 23-year-old immunocompetent drug addict presented with alternating sciatica over a period of several months, followed by multiple cranial nerve involvement in the setting of marked weight loss. The histopathologic examination of a leptomeningeal neurosurgical biopsy was required to establish the diagnosis of neuromeningeal infection with Candida albicans."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "This case report underlines diagnostic difficulties of candidal meningitis and reviews current therapeutic recommendations."
          }
        ]
      },
